 "chunkId": "senses-3",
 "label": "Understanding",
 "topic": "Senses & mind",
 "rationale": "The wakaru family: you get what something means, how someone feels, or the difference between two things.",
 "mixUps": "All three take ga + wakaru, never shitteru, which is knowing a fact; 'tell the difference' is wakaru, not iu. imi nai (it's pointless) is not imi wakannai (it makes no sense).",
 "targets": [
  {
   "id": "c-imi-ga-wakaru",
   "romaji": "imi ga wakaru",
   "polite": "imi ga wakarimasu",
   "english": "understand what something means; (negative) make no sense",
   "ja": "意味が分かる",
   "kana": "いみがわかる",
   "politeJa": "意味が分かります",
   "noun": "imi",
   "particle": "ga",
   "verb": "wakaru",
   "verbJa": "分かる",
   "verbSense": "understand, be clear; here the meaning gets through to you",
   "englishVerb": "make sense",
   "trap": "For 'that makes no sense', learners say imi ga nai, but imi nai means 'it's pointless' (yatte mo imi nai). Something that doesn't add up is imi wakannai: you can't get the meaning. wake wakannai and the slangy imifu say the same.",
   "partners": [
    "riyuu (the reason)",
    "kotoba (words, a language)",
    "setsumei (the explanation: setsumei wakatta?)"
   ],
   "flags": [],
   "use": "Mostly heard in the negative as a casual reaction, with ga dropped: imi wakannai (that makes no sense) about a rule, a joke or someone acting strangely. Aimed at what someone just said, it's blunt, close to 'you're not making sense'; with someone above you, ask dou iu imi desu ka? instead. imi wakaru? (you get what I mean?) checks that someone is following you.",
   "notes": "For 'oh, that makes sense', the usual reply is naruhodo; aa, imi wakatta means 'oh, now I get it'.",
   "example": {
    "romaji": "nani sore, imi wakannai.",
    "ja": "何それ、意味分かんない。",
    "english": "What? That makes no sense."
   },
   "examplePolite": {
    "romaji": "sumimasen, chotto imi ga yoku wakaranai n desu kedo.",
    "ja": "すみません、ちょっと意味がよく分からないんですけど。",
    "english": "Sorry, I don't quite follow what you mean."
   }
  },
  {
   "id": "c-kimochi-ga-wakaru",
   "romaji": "kimochi ga wakaru",
   "polite": "kimochi ga wakarimasu",
   "english": "know how someone feels",
   "ja": "気持ちが分かる",
   "kana": "きもちがわかる",
   "politeJa": "気持ちが分かります",
   "noun": "kimochi",
   "particle": "ga",
   "verb": "wakaru",
   "verbJa": "分かる",
   "verbSense": "understand; here, relate to how someone feels",
   "englishVerb": "know",
   "trap": "For 'I know how you feel', learners say kimochi o shitteru, but shiru is knowing a fact: kimi no kimochi shitteru means 'I know what your feelings are' (say, a crush). Relating to someone is wakaru: sono kimochi wakaru.",
   "partners": [
    "kanji (that feeling: sono kanji wakaru)",
    "tsurasa (how hard it is)",
    "kurou (what someone went through)"
   ],
   "flags": [],
   "use": "The everyday empathy line, with ga dropped: sono kimochi wakaru (I know how you feel), often flipped as wakaru, sono kimochi. With wa it sets up a 'but': kimochi wa wakaru kedo... (I get it, but...), the gentle way to disagree.",
   "notes": "hito no kimochi ga wakannai (has no idea how other people feel) is a common complaint about someone tactless. To a customer or someone senior it's okimochi: okimochi wa wakarimasu ga... (I understand, but...).",
   "example": {
    "romaji": "wakaru wakaru, sono kimochi.",
    "ja": "分かる分かる、その気持ち。",
    "english": "Oh, I totally know how you feel."
   },
   "examplePolite": {
    "romaji": "sono kimochi, yoku wakarimasu.",
    "ja": "その気持ち、よく分かります。",
    "english": "I know exactly how you feel."
   }
  },
  {
   "id": "c-chigai-ga-wakaru",
   "romaji": "chigai ga wakaru",
   "polite": "chigai ga wakarimasu",
   "english": "tell the difference",
   "ja": "違いが分かる",
   "kana": "ちがいがわかる",
   "politeJa": "違いが分かります",
   "noun": "chigai",
   "particle": "ga",
   "verb": "wakaru",
   "verbJa": "分かる",
   "verbSense": "understand, be clear; here you can make out the difference",
   "englishVerb": "tell",
   "trap": "Learners put o on it (chigai o wakaranai), but wakaru takes ga, or drops it in speech: chigai wakannai. 'Tell' isn't iu or oshieru here either: chigai o oshiete asks someone to explain the difference. kubetsu tsukanai (can't tell them apart) also works.",
   "partners": [
    "aji (aji ga wakaru: have a palate)",
    "yosa (yosa ga wakaru: see what's good about it)"
   ],
   "flags": [],
   "use": "Mostly negative, after tasting or comparing two things: zenzen chigai wakannai (I can't tell the difference at all), with ga dropped. The things compared take no: kono futatsu no chigai.",
   "notes": "The verb chigau does much of this work too: nani ga chigau no? (what's the difference?). In the positive it praises a discerning palate: chigai ga wakaru hito (someone who can tell good from ordinary).",
   "example": {
    "romaji": "shoujiki, zenzen chigai wakannai.",
    "ja": "正直、全然違い分かんない。",
    "english": "Honestly, I can't tell the difference at all."
   },
   "examplePolite": {
    "romaji": "shoujiki, zenzen chigai ga wakarimasen.",
    "ja": "正直、全然違いが分かりません。",
    "english": "Honestly, I can't tell the difference at all."
   }
  }
 ],
